> A residual does not prove a missing extreme

For diag(1,2,9), start(1,0,0) spans only the eigenvalue1 direction. It produces
an exact eigenpair with zero residual while completely missing the global
largest eigenvalue9. Starting with all ones admits every direction. Scaling the
matrix by1e−14 makes the historical absolute stopping threshold trigger after a
nonzero first residual, showing why that threshold is not an exact
invariant-subspace or relative-accuracy certificate.

In exact symmetric arithmetic, a formed nonzero y satisfies
distance(theta,spectrum(A))<=||A*y−theta*y||/||y||. This asserts proximity to
some eigenvalue, not that it is the largest/smallest or that every spectral
direction was present. Native widened norms are numerical observations rather
than exact-real bounds with certified rounding error.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lanczos_limits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| case ordinal | matrix scale | start direction | actual basis vectors | actual stop code | " "actual largest returned Ritz divided by scale | largest explicit residual over vector norm " "divided by scale |\n| ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    const std::vector<double> scales = {1, 1e-14, 1, 1e14};
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < scales.size(); ++i) {
        const double s = scales[i];
        const std::vector<std::vector<double>> A = {{s, 0, 0}, {0, 2 * s, 0},
                                                    {0, 0, 9 * s}};
        const std::vector<double> b =
            i == 0 ? std::vector<double>{1, 0, 0}
            : std::vector<double>{1, 1, 1};
        const auto t = dm::algorithms::lanczos_trace(A, b, 3);
        const auto r = dm::algorithms::lanczos_ritz_analysis(A, t.result);
        std::print("| {} | {:.3e} | {} | {} | {} | {:.12f} | {:.12e} |\n", i, s,
                   i == 0 ? "first axis" : "all ones", t.result.alpha.size(),
                   int(t.stop), r.values.front() / s,
                   r.residual_norms.front() / r.vector_norms.front() / s);
        x.push_back(i);
        y.push_back(r.values.front() / s);
    }
    std::print("\nActual zero-start basis vectors {}.\n",
               dm::algorithms::lanczos_trace({{1}}, {0}, 4).result.alpha.size());
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, y, "returned", {37, 99, 235}, 6).line({0, 3}, {9, 9},
                                                      "global max",
                                                      {220, 38, 38}, 2).y_limits(-.5, 12).title("Start components and absolute stopping change the result").x_label("complete case ordinal, see table").y_label("largest returned Ritz value divided by scale").save_svg("build/lanczos_limits.svg");
}
```
